<<~ ahu #/rank-3-epoch-frontier>>

!! RANK 3 — The epoch as a monotone forward frontier / the no-flag-day engine roll

We are about to design the coexistence span for rolling the engine on a live mesh. The field proves this is a ''tagging discipline'', six independent ways.

''VINDICATION.''

* ''Veilid `CryptoKind`'' typed keys (fourcc-tagged: VLD0=Ed25519, VLD1=ML-DSA): ''"every new version is supported alongside the old ones,"'' separate routing table per cryptosystem, reads auto-select, ''writes default to newest (lazy migration / best-crypto write-back)'' (https://veilid.com/how-it-works/cryptography/). This is the closest field analog to our engine-CID slow ratchet: version each unit, route by its tag, write forward, both coexist.
* ''UCAN'' per-subspec versioned envelope tags (`ucan/dlg@1.0.0`) ''"enable future protocol evolution without flag-days"'' (https://github.com/ucan-wg/spec).
* ''ATProto Lexicon'': backward-compatible changes only (new fields optional, types can't change), and ''"if larger breaking changes are necessary, a new Lexicon name must be used,"'' with ''fail-open permissive readers'' (ignore unexpected fields) giving coexistence (https://atproto.com/specs/lexicon). This is our "breaking = a new object (new engine CID), never mutate the old meaning" + our graceful-parsing stance, deployed.
* ''Matrix room-version upgrade'': a breaking version spawns a NEW room id, linked by `m.room.tombstone` (`replacement_room`) + the new room's `predecessor` — clients ''follow the chain forward'' on their own schedule, no flag day (MSC1501). This is our `sealLineage` hash-linked chain head (each link names its parent) as a migration mechanic.
* ''DXOS epochs'': peers cooperatively agree to a new epoch that collapses history into a compact baseline without breaking the running stream (https://blog.dxos.org/decentralized-schema-changes-and-data-migrations/) — vindicates our borrowed "compaction epoch" (bounds state-history growth, never who-may-act) for `bag-compact`.
* ''BeeKEM (Keyhive — we depend on it)'' vindicates `epoch-binding-surfaces.mem` at primary source: concurrent CGKA rolls ''blank-and-merge rather than pick a winner'', and the security parameter ''κ trades forward secrecy for concurrent-secret access'' — exactly our ruling that "carrying our max-register fence onto a CGKA epoch costs forward secrecy" (https://www.inkandswitch.com/keyhive/notebook/).

''EXPOSURE / where the field is honest about the cost.'' Iroh chose a deliberate ''pre-1.0 flag-day'' for breaking wire changes (ALPN gives per-protocol coexistence only afterward); Farcaster's ''Snapchain migration was a genuine network-wide flag-day''. Both are the cliff we are trying to avoid — and both are the systems with the most central coordination, which is how they ''could'' schedule a flag day. Our refusal of a global roster is what forces (and permits) the coexistence span.

''NEGENTROPY TO ADOPT.'' Formalize the engine roll as ''Veilid's model'': the engine CID is a `CryptoKind`-style tag; a vessel routes by the engine tag it carries, reads accept both engine epochs during the span, writes forward to the new engine, and the graft edge (`device-delegation` preimage) carries a Matrix-style `predecessor` pointer to the prior `hearthTrueName`. The span ends by non-renewal, per the meme's own law.

''ANTI-PATTERN.'' ''Willow's prefix-pruning + hostile-clock'': a newer short-path entry silently tombstones a whole subtree, and a skewed/hostile clock weaponizes "newer" (https://willowprotocol.org/specs/data-model/). This is a live, measured instance of exactly why `epoch-binding-surfaces.mem` KILLED the rolling-window shape ("a set answers ''is this listed'', never ''is this newer''") and why we hold "correct even if all timestamps were zero." Do not let any migration marker resolve on a self-asserted time.

''Folds into:'' `epoch-binding-surfaces.mem` (the tagging-discipline vindications; BeeKEM κ at source; Willow hostile-clock as the anti-pattern that confirms the window-death ruling); the coexistence-span design.

